(7,45,"This at first seemed a great misfortune, for I knew a one-legged",
"The Tin Woodman tells how the Witch's spell made his axe cut off his leg, arms and head. Each time the tinsmith made a new part out of tin.",
"The Tin Woodman says, \"At first this seemed a great bad luck, because I knew that a man with one leg could not do very well as a woodchopper. So I went to a tinsmith and had him make me a new leg out of tin. The leg worked very well once I was used to it. But what I did made the Wicked Witch of the East angry, because she had promised the old woman that I should not marry the pretty Munchkin girl. When I began chopping again, my axe slipped and cut off my right leg. Again I went to the tinsmith, and again he made me a leg out of tin. After this the enchanted axe cut off my arms, one after the other. But I did not give up, and I had them put back with tin ones. Then the Wicked Witch made the axe slip and cut off my head, and at first I thought that was the end of me. But the tinsmith happened to come along, and he made me a new head out of tin.\"",
["misfortune","woodchopper","tinsmith","enchanted","daunted","replaced"]),
(7,47,"My body shone so brightly in the sun that I felt very proud",
"The Tin Woodman's joints rusted in the rain and he stood still for a year. He decided that the loss of his heart was the worst thing, so he wants to ask Oz for one.",
"The Tin Woodman says, \"My body shone so brightly in the sun that I felt very proud of it. It did not matter now if my axe slipped, because it could not cut me. There was only one danger, that my joints would rust. But I kept an oilcan in my cottage and took care to oil myself whenever I needed it. However, there came a day when I forgot to do this. I was caught in a rainstorm, and before I thought of the danger my joints had rusted. I was left to stand in the woods until you came to help me. It was a terrible thing to go through, but during the year I stood there I had time to think that the greatest loss I had known was the loss of my heart. While I was in love I was the happiest man on earth. But no one can love who has not a heart, and so I have decided to ask Oz to give me one. If he does, I will go back to the Munchkin maiden and marry her.\"",
["oilcan","rusted","undergo","resolved","maiden"]),
(8,25,"It’s a mystery,” replied the Lion.",
"The Lion says that he was born a coward. Animals think he is brave because he roars, and they run away, but he would run from the bigger animals if they fought him.",
"\"It is a mystery,\" said the Lion. \"I suppose I was born that way. All the other animals in the forest naturally expect me to be brave, because the Lion is thought everywhere to be the King of Beasts. I learned that if I roared very loudly, every living thing was frightened and got out of my way. Whenever I have met a man I have been very scared. But I just roared at him, and he has always run away as fast as he could go. If the elephants and the tigers and the bears had ever tried to fight me, I should have run myself, because I am such a coward. But just as soon as they hear me roar, they all try to get away from me, and of course I let them go.\"",
["mystery","coward","roared"]),
(9,7,"This was to be an eventful day for the travelers.",
"The travelers come to a very wide, deep ditch with sharp rocks at the bottom. The sides are too steep to climb, so it seems that their journey must end.",
"This was to be a day full of events for the travelers. They had hardly been walking for an hour when they saw in front of them a great ditch. It crossed the road and cut the forest in two as far as they could see on either side. It was a very wide ditch, and when they crept up to the edge and looked into it, they could see that it was also very deep. There were many big, jagged rocks at the bottom. The sides were so steep that none of them could climb down, and for a moment it seemed that their journey must end.",
["eventful","ditch","jagged","steep"]),
(10,45,"They now came upon more and more of the big scarlet poppies",
"The friends walk into a big field of red poppies. The smell of these flowers puts people to sleep, and Dorothy does not know it, so she feels she must sit down and sleep.",
"Now they came on more and more of the big scarlet poppies, and fewer and fewer of the other flowers. Soon they found themselves in the middle of a great meadow of poppies. Now it is well known that when there are many of these flowers together, their smell is so strong that anyone who breathes it falls asleep. If the sleeper is not carried away from the smell of the flowers, he sleeps on and on forever. But Dorothy did not know this, and she could not get away from the bright red flowers that were everywhere about. So soon her eyes grew heavy, and she felt she must sit down to rest and to sleep.",
["scarlet","poppies","odor","scent","presently"]),
(11,3,"The Tin Woodman was about to reply when he heard a low growl",
"The Tin Woodman sees a Wildcat chase a little field mouse. He has no heart, but he knows it is wrong to kill such a harmless animal.",
"The Tin Woodman was about to answer when he heard a low growl. He turned his head, which worked very well on hinges, and saw a strange beast come bounding over the grass toward them. It was a great yellow Wildcat. The Woodman thought it must be chasing something, because its ears were lying close to its head and its mouth was wide open. It showed two rows of ugly teeth, and its red eyes glowed like balls of fire. As it came nearer, the Tin Woodman saw that a little gray field mouse was running in front of the beast. Although he had no heart, he knew it was wrong for the Wildcat to try to kill such a pretty, harmless creature.",
["growl","hinges","bounding","wildcat","harmless"]),
(12,26,"That is hard to tell,” said the man thoughtfully.",
"The man says that Oz can take any form he wants, so people say different things about him. No living person can tell what Oz looks like in his own form.",
"\"That is hard to tell,\" said the man, thinking. \"You see, Oz is a Great Wizard, and can take any form he wishes. So some say he looks like a bird, and some say he looks like an elephant, and some say he looks like a cat. To others he looks like a beautiful fairy, or a brownie, or any other form that he likes. But who the real Oz is, when he is in his own form, no living person can tell.\"",
["brownie"]),
(12,55,"He opened the big box, and Dorothy saw that it was filled",
"The Guardian puts green spectacles on Dorothy and locks them with a little key. She does not mind, because the glare of the Emerald City would hurt her eyes.",
"He opened the big box, and Dorothy saw that it was filled with spectacles of every size and shape. All of them had green glasses in them. The Guardian of the Gates found a pair that would just fit Dorothy and put them over her eyes. Two golden bands were fastened to them and passed around the back of her head. There they were locked together by a little key at the end of a chain that the Guardian of the Gates wore around his neck. When they were on, Dorothy could not take them off if she had wished. But of course she did not wish to be blinded by the glare of the Emerald City, so she said nothing.",
["spectacles","glare","guardian"]),
(13,2,"Even with eyes protected by the green spectacles",
"The Emerald City is so bright that even with green spectacles the friends are dazzled. The houses, streets and windows are all green, and emeralds shine everywhere.",
"Dorothy and her friends had eyes protected by the green spectacles, but at first they were still dazzled by the brightness of the wonderful City. The streets were lined with beautiful houses, all built of green marble and covered everywhere with shining emeralds. They walked over a pavement of the same green marble. Where the blocks were joined together were rows of emeralds, set close together and shining in the brightness of the sun. The window panes were of green glass. Even the sky above the City had a green color, and the rays of the sun were green.",
["dazzled","brilliancy","studded","pavement","tint","emeralds"]),
(13,70,"But when the Woodman entered the great Throne Room",
"Oz shows himself to the Tin Woodman as a terrible Beast with five eyes, five arms and five legs. The Woodman is not afraid, because he is made of tin and has no heart.",
"When the Woodman went into the great Throne Room, he saw neither the Head nor the Lady, because Oz had taken the shape of a most terrible Beast. It was nearly as big as an elephant, and the green throne seemed hardly strong enough to hold its weight. The Beast had a head like that of a rhinoceros, but there were five eyes in its face. There were five long arms growing out of its body, and it also had five long, thin legs. Thick, woolly hair covered every part of it. A more frightening monster could not be imagined. It was lucky that the Tin Woodman had no heart at that moment, because it would have beat loud and fast from fear. But he was only tin, so he was not at all afraid, although he was very disappointed.",
["rhinoceros","dreadful","fortunate","terror","woolly"]),
